---
import Tool from '../layouts/Tool.astro';
import TextToolFrame from '../components/TextToolFrame.astro';
import ExcelToCsvTool from '../components/ExcelToCsvTool.astro';
import JsonToExcelTool from '../components/JsonToExcelTool.astro';
import JsonToCsvTool from '../components/JsonToCsvTool.astro';
import XmlToCsvTool from '../components/XmlToCsvTool.astro';
import XmlToJsonTool from '../components/XmlToJsonTool.astro';
import CsvToSqlTool from '../components/CsvToSqlTool.astro';
import JsonBeautifierTool from '../components/JsonBeautifierTool.astro';
import { tools, publishedToolLocales } from '../data/tools';
import { t, type TranslationKey } from '../i18n/utils';
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return Object.values(tools).flatMap(tool => publishedToolLocales(tool).map(([locale, entry]) => ({ params: { tool: entry.path.replace(/^\/|\/$/g, '') }, props: { locale, entry, tool } })));
}
const { locale, entry, tool } = Astro.props;
const alternates = Object.fromEntries(publishedToolLocales(tool).map(([lang, item]) => [lang, item.path]));
const excelRows: [TranslationKey, TranslationKey][] = [
  ['excel.howNumbers', 'excel.howNumbersResult'],
  ['excel.howDates', 'excel.howDatesResult'],
  ['excel.howFormulas', 'excel.howFormulasResult'],
  ['excel.howMerged', 'excel.howMergedResult'],
  ['excel.howHidden', 'excel.howHiddenResult'],
  ['excel.howFormatting', 'excel.howFormattingResult'],
];
const jsonExcelRows: [TranslationKey, TranslationKey][] = [
  ['jsonExcel.howArray', 'jsonExcel.howArrayResult'],
  ['jsonExcel.howObject', 'jsonExcel.howObjectResult'],
  ['jsonExcel.howList', 'jsonExcel.howListResult'],
  ['jsonExcel.howSimpleList', 'jsonExcel.howSimpleListResult'],
  ['jsonExcel.howRoot', 'jsonExcel.howRootResult'],
  ['jsonExcel.howGrid', 'jsonExcel.howGridResult'],
];
---
<Tool {locale} {entry} category={tool.category} {alternates}>
  <main class="wrap">
    {tool.id === 'json-beautifier' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>Make JSON easier to read.</strong> Paste text or choose a file, pick an indentation style, and copy or download the formatted result. Everything runs in your browser.</p><p class="caption">.json and .txt files up to 10 MB</p></div></div>
      <JsonBeautifierTool />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title"><div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">How it works</h2></div><div class="how-copy">
        <p>The beautifier checks that your input is valid JSON, then adds line breaks and indentation. Choose 2 spaces, 4 spaces or tabs. Compact removes optional whitespace when you need a smaller text file. The output can be copied or downloaded as .json.</p>
        <p>Objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans and null are accepted at the top level. Integer digits larger than JavaScript can safely represent are preserved. As with JSON parsing generally, duplicate object keys collapse to their last value, and object keys made only of integer digits may be reordered.</p>
        <p>For invalid JSON, the message points to a line and column and suggests a fix for common problems such as trailing commas, single quotes and unquoted keys. JSON Lines, comments and JSON with trailing commas are not accepted as standard JSON. Files up to 10 MB are processed locally in a Web Worker; large files may use substantial memory on a phone.</p>
      </div></section>
    </>}
    {tool.id === 'csv-to-sql' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>Turn CSV rows into SQL INSERT statements.</strong> Paste data or choose a file, select your database, then copy or download the generated SQL. Conversion happens in your browser.</p><p class="caption">.csv, .tsv and .txt files up to 10 MB</p></div></div>
      <CsvToSqlTool />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title"><div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">How CSV becomes SQL</h2></div><div class="how-copy">
        <p>The first CSV row becomes column names by default. Each remaining row becomes values in an INSERT statement. You can include a CREATE TABLE statement, combine up to 500 rows per INSERT, and choose PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite or SQL Server quoting.</p>
        <h3>What happens to values</h3>
        <div class="table-scroll"><table class="spec-table mapping-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">CSV input</th><th scope="col">SQL output</th></tr></thead><tbody>
          <tr><td><code>001</code></td><td><code>'001'</code> by default, so leading zeros survive. Turn on type detection only if the destination column expects a number.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>O'Neil</code></td><td><code>'O''Neil'</code>. Apostrophes are escaped, and identifiers are quoted for the chosen database.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>An empty field</td><td><code>''</code> by default. Choose “Convert empty fields to NULL” when missing values should be SQL <code>NULL</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A quoted field with a comma or line break</td><td>One text value. The CSV parser keeps quoted fields together and decodes doubled quotes.</td></tr>
        </tbody></table></div>
        <p>Auto detect checks comma, semicolon, tab and pipe separators. If the preview looks wrong, choose the separator explicitly. Blank and duplicate column names get unique names so the SQL remains usable. Type detection recognizes booleans and conservative integer or decimal values; dates stay text because date formats vary by database.</p>
        <p>The full result is available through Copy SQL or Download .sql. The on-page SQL preview shows its beginning when output is long, and the table preview shows the first 100 rows and 30 columns. Large files can take longer on a phone. For MySQL, backslashes are escaped using its usual string syntax; review that output if your server uses <code>NO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES</code>.</p>
      </div></section>
    </>}
    {tool.id === 'json-to-html' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>{t('tool.leadStrong', locale)}</strong> {t('tool.lead', locale)}</p><p class="caption">{t('tool.caption', locale)}</p></div></div>
      <TextToolFrame {locale} />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">{t('how.title', locale)}</h2></div>
        <div class="how-copy">
          <p>{t('how.intro', locale)}</p>
          <h3>{t('how.mapTitle', locale)}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="spec-table mapping-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">{t('how.yourJson', locale)}</th><th scope="col">{t('how.youGet', locale)}</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>{t('how.arrayObjects', locale)}</td><td>{t('how.arrayObjectsResult', locale)}</td></tr>
              <tr><td>{t('how.object', locale)}</td><td>{t('how.objectResult', locale)}</td></tr>
              <tr><td>{t('how.nested', locale)}</td><td>{t('how.nestedResult', locale)}</td></tr>
              <tr><td>{t('how.simple', locale)}</td><td>{t('how.simpleResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            </tbody></table></div>
          <p>{t('how.layouts', locale)}</p><p>{t('how.outputs', locale)}</p><p>{t('how.limits', locale)}</p><p>{t('how.errors', locale)}</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </>}
    {tool.id === 'excel-to-csv' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>{t('excel.leadStrong', locale)}</strong> {t('excel.lead', locale)}</p><p class="caption">{t('excel.caption', locale)}</p></div></div>
      <ExcelToCsvTool {locale} />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">{t('how.title', locale)}</h2></div>
        <div class="how-copy">
          <p>{t('excel.howIntro', locale)}</p>
          <h3>{t('excel.howMapTitle', locale)}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="spec-table mapping-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">{t('excel.howInWorkbook', locale)}</th><th scope="col">{t('excel.howInCsv', locale)}</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{excelRows.map(([what, result]) => <tr><td>{t(what, locale)}</td><td>{t(result, locale)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
          <p>{t('excel.howSeparators', locale)}</p><p>{t('excel.howEncoding', locale)}</p><p>{t('excel.howLimits', locale)}</p><p>{t('excel.howErrors', locale)}</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </>}
    {tool.id === 'json-to-excel' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>{t('jsonExcel.leadStrong', locale)}</strong> {t('jsonExcel.lead', locale)}</p><p class="caption">{t('jsonExcel.caption', locale)}</p></div></div>
      <JsonToExcelTool {locale} />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">{t('how.title', locale)}</h2></div>
        <div class="how-copy">
          <p>{t('jsonExcel.howIntro', locale)}</p>
          <h3>{t('jsonExcel.howMapTitle', locale)}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="spec-table mapping-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">{t('jsonExcel.howYourJson', locale)}</th><th scope="col">{t('jsonExcel.howInWorkbook', locale)}</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{jsonExcelRows.map(([what, result]) => <tr><td>{t(what, locale)}</td><td>{t(result, locale)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
          <p>{t('jsonExcel.howValues', locale)}</p><p>{t('jsonExcel.howNested', locale)}</p><p>{t('jsonExcel.howLimits', locale)}</p><p>{t('jsonExcel.howErrors', locale)}</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </>}
    {tool.id === 'json-to-csv' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>{t('jsonCsv.leadStrong', locale)}</strong> {t('jsonCsv.lead', locale)}</p><p class="caption">{t('jsonCsv.caption', locale)}</p></div></div>
      <JsonToCsvTool {locale} />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">{t('how.title', locale)}</h2></div>
        <div class="how-copy">
          <p>{t('jsonCsv.howIntro', locale)}</p>
          <h3>{t('jsonCsv.howMapTitle', locale)}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="spec-table mapping-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">{t('jsonCsv.howYourJson', locale)}</th><th scope="col">{t('jsonCsv.howInCsv', locale)}</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>{t('jsonCsv.howArray', locale)}</td><td>{t('jsonCsv.howArrayResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>{t('jsonCsv.howObject', locale)}</td><td>{t('jsonCsv.howObjectResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>{t('jsonCsv.howNested', locale)}</td><td>{t('jsonCsv.howNestedResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>{t('jsonCsv.howList', locale)}</td><td>{t('jsonCsv.howListResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>{t('jsonCsv.howGrid', locale)}</td><td>{t('jsonCsv.howGridResult', locale)}</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <p>{t('jsonCsv.howFormat', locale)}</p><p>{t('jsonCsv.howSafety', locale)}</p><p>{t('jsonCsv.howLimits', locale)}</p>
          <p><a href="/guides/json-to-csv/">{t('jsonCsv.guideLink', locale)}</a></p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </>}
    {tool.id === 'xml-to-csv' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>{t('xmlCsv.leadStrong', locale)}</strong> {t('xmlCsv.lead', locale)}</p><p class="caption">{t('xmlCsv.caption', locale)}</p></div></div>
      <XmlToCsvTool {locale} />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">{t('how.title', locale)}</h2></div>
        <div class="how-copy">
          <p>{t('xmlCsv.howIntro', locale)}</p>
          <h3>{t('xmlCsv.howMapTitle', locale)}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="spec-table mapping-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">{t('xmlCsv.howYourXml', locale)}</th><th scope="col">{t('xmlCsv.howInCsv', locale)}</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>{t('xmlCsv.howRecords', locale)}</td><td>{t('xmlCsv.howRecordsResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>{t('xmlCsv.howAttributes', locale)}</td><td>{t('xmlCsv.howAttributesResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>{t('xmlCsv.howNested', locale)}</td><td>{t('xmlCsv.howNestedResult', locale)}</td></tr>
            <tr><td>{t('xmlCsv.howRepeated', locale)}</td><td>{t('xmlCsv.howRepeatedResult', locale)}</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <h3>{t('xmlCsv.howStepsTitle', locale)}</h3>
          <p>{t('xmlCsv.howSteps', locale)}</p>
          <p>{t('xmlCsv.howFormat', locale)}</p><p>{t('xmlCsv.howSafety', locale)}</p><p>{t('xmlCsv.howLimits', locale)}</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </>}
    {tool.id === 'xml-to-json' && <>
      <div class="hero"><h1 class="page-title">{entry.h1}</h1><div class="hero-row"><p class="lead"><strong>{t('xmlJson.leadStrong', locale)}</strong> {t('xmlJson.lead', locale)}</p><p class="caption">{t('xmlJson.caption', locale)}</p></div></div>
      <XmlToJsonTool />
      <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
        <div class="sec-head"><h2 id="how-title">How XML becomes JSON</h2></div>
        <div class="how-copy">
          <p>Paste XML or choose a local file, then convert it into valid JSON. The document root remains the top-level key. Conversion runs in a Web Worker in your browser; the XML is not uploaded.</p>
          <h3>Mapping rules</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="spec-table mapping-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">XML input</th><th scope="col">JSON output</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td><code>&lt;name&gt;Maya&lt;/name&gt;</code></td><td><code>"name": "Maya"</code>. Text values stay strings, including numbers such as <code>001</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>&lt;order id="A-104"&gt;</code></td><td>The attribute becomes <code>"@_id": "A-104"</code>. Choose <code>@id</code> if your importer uses that convention.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Two or more sibling <code>&lt;tag&gt;</code> elements</td><td>They become a <code>"tag"</code> array, in source order. Turn on “Always use arrays” for consistent arrays even when one element appears.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Text and child elements together</td><td><code>#text</code> holds the combined text; <code>#content</code> keeps text and child elements in source order.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An empty element or CDATA</td><td><code>&lt;empty/&gt;</code> becomes an empty string. CDATA becomes ordinary text. XML entities are decoded.</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <h3>Example</h3>
          <p><code>&lt;person id="7"&gt;&lt;name&gt;Maya&lt;/name&gt;&lt;name&gt;Luis&lt;/name&gt;&lt;/person&gt;</code> becomes <code>{'{"person":{"@_id":"7","name":["Maya","Luis"]}}'}</code>. Use Pretty print to make the output easier to read, or turn it off for compact JSON.</p>
          <p>XML has attributes, namespaces, comments and mixed content; JSON has no single standard way to represent them. This converter keeps namespace prefixes in names and omits comments and processing instructions. Simple text and <code>#text</code> are trimmed; <code>#content</code> retains the spaces around inline elements. It rejects DOCTYPE declarations and malformed XML, with a line and column for syntax errors.</p>
          <p>Choose a .xml or .txt file up to 10 MB. Copy the complete JSON or download a .json file. Large inputs can take longer on a phone. Need spreadsheet rows instead? Try <a href="/xml-to-csv/">XML to CSV</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </>}
  </main>
</Tool>
